  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-141062",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that the logarithms share the same base. \n\nFirst, we apply the power rule to the first term to make the coefficient in front of each logarithm the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{8}⁡(16{x}^{4})-{log}_{8}⁡(\\frac{1}{2x}) & =1 \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡({(16{x}^{4})}^{1/2})-{log}_{8}⁡(\\frac{1}{2x}) & =1 \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡(4{x}^{2})-{log}_{8}⁡(\\frac{1}{2x}) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we combine the logarithms using the quotient rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡(\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{(\\frac{1}{2x})}) & =1 \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡(8{x}^{3}) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting this equation in its equivalent exponential form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{3} & ={8}^{1} \\\\ 8{x}^{3} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve this rational equation for $x$ using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{8} & =\\frac{8}{8} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{8}{x}^{3}}{\\cancel{8}} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =1 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for an extraneous solution by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{8}⁡(16\\cdot {1}^{4})-{log}_{8}⁡(\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 1}) & \\overset{?}{=}1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{log}_{8}⁡16-{log}_{8}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}1 \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡{16}^{1/2}-{log}_{8}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}1 \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡4-{log}_{8}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}1 \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡(\\frac{4}{(\\frac{1}{2})}) & \\overset{?}{=}1 \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡(8) & \\overset{?}{=}1 \\\\ 1 & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$x=1$ only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
